  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nThe breezy/gusty northwest winds across the region this afternoon \nwill become light and variable this evening as surface high pressure \nbuilds in. Skies look to be mostly clear at least for a good portion \nof the night, but there are indications in high clouds thickening at \nleast over western/central SD by 12Z Tuesday. Given what appears to \nbe a fairly good radiational cooling night, decided to blend in \nlower-end NBM10 guidance for temps, while dropping an additional \ndegree or two in the James River valley. There are also hints of fog \ndevelopment as well, mainly in the James and Big Sioux valleys. Not \na solid signal amongst model visibility grids, but seemed enough to \nmention just \"patchy\" wording. Soundings are rather dry right off \nthe surface, so may be more ground/shallow fog potential than \nanything else.  \n\nAnother mild day in store for Tuesday, although temps aloft at 850mb \nare slightly cooler, and resulting high temp forecast is more into \nthe mid/upper 50s to low 60s. Decided to blend in about 50% NBM90 \ntemps as well given the trend recently. Did not go all-in with NBM90 \ngiven what models are showing to be some mid/high level cloudiness \nacross the region which may put a damper of full heating potential.\n\nFocus then shifts to the weak surface low moving southeast through \nthe region Tuesday into Tuesday night. Surface low track is \nbasically right over the CWA, with apparent best precip chances over \nthe favorable northern side of the low. Given that, REFS probability \nfor measurable (>=0.01in) is about 20-50% along and north of a line \nfrom MBG to ATY. Inherited NBM PoPs still show about 20-30% chances \nin the same area, although did some massaging to the grids to fill \nholes and expand southward just a tad. Any rainfall that does \nmaterialize will be very light and likely be less than 0.25in.\n\nColder temps still in store for Wednesday and especially Thursday as \na lobe of colder air aloft moves south out of Canada and into the \nNorthern Plains/Great Lakes region. The Dakotas is on the western \nend of the brunt of cold, but will still see 850mb temps likely \ndropping below zero C across the eastern CWA. Grand Ensemble 850mb \ntemps still showing -2C to 0C from the James River eastward on \nThursday. Forecast highs on Thursday are still in the upper 30s for \neastern portions of the CWA. Still seeing signs of a warming trend \nagain (although perhaps delayed 12-24hrs) Saturday and especially by \nSunday/Monday with highs forecast back in the 50s to near 60 degrees.",
